  "dom_id": "step-q346054",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Let $f$ be a function such that ${f}^{\u2032}(x)={x}^{3}-1$ and $f(-1)=2.$ Use the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=-1$ to estimate the value of $f(-0.6).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$f(-0.6)\u2248$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=-1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{\u2032}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(-1)+{f}^{\u2032}(-1)(x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $f(-1)=2,$ and since ${f}^{\u2032}(x)={x}^{3}-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2032}(-1)=(-1)^{3}-1=-2.\n$$\n\nSo, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\nL(x)=2-2(x+1).\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(-0.6)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=-0.6.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-0.6) & \u2248L(-0.6) \\\\  & =2-2(-0.6+1) \\\\  & =2-2(0.4) \\\\  & =2-0.8 \\\\  & =1.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
